.TH FRAMEGRAB 1
.SH NAME
framegrab \- run a graphical program for a moment and write down what it drew
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B framegrab
[
.B -n
.I ms
] [
.B -o
.I file
]
.I module
[
.I arg ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Framegrab
loads
.I module
as a
.B Command
in a process group of its own, gives it a draw context, waits
.I ms
milliseconds, reads the display back, kills it, and writes the pixels to
.I file
as raw bytes in the display's own format.
.I Ms
defaults to 1500 and
.I file
to
.BR framegrab.raw .
A
.I module
without a
.B .dis
suffix is looked for in
.BR /dis .
.PP
There was no way to do this.
The demos in
.B /dis/temple
open a window, draw, and keep drawing; the only way to find out whether
the picture was right was to be in front of the screen.
That is why
.B /doc/draw3d-adoption-plan.md
had nine ports whose pictures "have not been checked" \- the evidence
for them was a submission counter and a pixel count taken on the
software path, and neither says the GPU drew the right thing.
.PP
With no window manager the display
.I is
what the program drew into, so what comes back is the frame.
.SS Comparing two providers without touching a port
The ports name their 3-D provider by path, and a path is a name in a
name space, so the other one can be bound over it:
.IP
.EX
bind /dis/math/draw3d.dis /dis/math/draw3ddev.dis
framegrab -n 1800 -o soft.raw temple/rotatetank
.EE
.PP
and again in a fresh name space without the bind.
No source changes, and the two runs are otherwise identical.
.SS What it prints
Three lines: the size and depth, some numbers about the pixels, and
where they went.
The numbers are the pixels that are not the background, the pixels that
are nearly white, and a hash of the whole frame.
The background is read from the corner rather than written down, because
a demo may clear to anything.
.PP
The hash is the useful one for a fault that comes and goes: two runs
that differ have drawn different frames, and two that agree
.I byte for byte
have drawn the same one, which for a demo that animates means neither of
them drew anything at all.
.SH EXAMPLE
.B rotatetank
through each provider, five and six runs:
.IP
.EX
software  20461 20461 20465 20448 20459
device    14503 20031 14503 19960 14503 14503
.EE
.PP
14503 is the window chrome and the caption with nothing in between, and
every one of those runs hashes identically.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/framegrab.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw3dbench (1),
.IR gorge (1),
.IR bind (1)
.PP
.B /doc/draw3d-adoption-plan.md
.SH BUGS
It reads the display's image, which is the software screen.
A present straight to a drawable that never writes that image back is
invisible to this, so a blank result means "a readback sees nothing",
which is what any in-process check and any port that composites its
window would also see \- but it is not proof that the screen was blank.
Deciding that still wants a camera.
.PP
The wait is wall-clock, so a demo that animates is caught at whatever
moment it is in.
Compare several runs rather than one.
.PP
It gives the module no keyboard and no pointer, so a demo that needs a
keypress to get past a title page never gets there.
